1
00:00:40,351 --> 00:00:41,811
Thors.

2
00:00:48,731 --> 00:00:49,731
Helga.

3
00:00:57,111 --> 00:00:58,201
Thors.

4
00:01:23,221 --> 00:01:26,441
YEAR 987 HJÖRUNGAVÁGR

5
00:01:28,351 --> 00:01:32,321
Why were you just standing there?
Are you feeling unwell?

6
00:01:32,401 --> 00:01:34,441
Sorry. I dozed off.

7
00:01:37,451 --> 00:01:41,621
We're too spread out. We won't be able
to hold off their second wave.

8
00:01:42,791 --> 00:01:47,871
Thorkell, when Floki approaches,
bring up the frontline.

9
00:01:48,211 --> 00:01:50,381
You mean that blockhead?

10
00:01:51,041 --> 00:01:53,171
I don't mind. What about you?

11
00:01:53,921 --> 00:01:55,421
I'm going to stop the enemy.

12
00:01:56,381 --> 00:01:58,051
Stop? What do you mean?

13
00:01:58,681 --> 00:02:00,391
How are you going to get there?

14
00:02:03,261 --> 00:02:06,811
I see. You're nimble.

15
00:02:06,891 --> 00:02:08,441
Wait. You're sneaking off alone?

16
00:02:48,731 --> 00:02:50,981
Maria. Sorry...

17
00:05:56,501 --> 00:05:59,541
SOMEWHERE NOT HERE

18
00:06:20,191 --> 00:06:23,611
YEAR 1002 ICELAND

19
00:06:41,751 --> 00:06:44,631
Thorfinn, can you feed the sheep and milk the cow?

20
00:06:44,711 --> 00:06:45,711
Okay.

21
00:07:30,841 --> 00:07:32,841
After leaving Markland,

22
00:07:32,931 --> 00:07:35,891
our ship sailed further south.

23
00:07:36,261 --> 00:07:38,311
"There are better lands."

24
00:07:38,971 --> 00:07:41,731
That's what the wind was telling us.

25
00:07:50,741 --> 00:07:52,821
And then we found it.

26
00:07:55,321 --> 00:07:57,241
The new continent.

27
00:08:04,001 --> 00:08:08,091
A new world where fruit grows
and grassy plains cover the land.

28
00:08:09,301 --> 00:08:11,721
I named that land.

29
00:08:19,601 --> 00:08:23,481
The name of that land is Vinland.

30
00:08:26,941 --> 00:08:31,991
So how about it, boys?
Have a better opinion of me, Leif Ericson, now?

31
00:08:32,321 --> 00:08:34,741
You're amazing, Leif.

32
00:08:35,031 --> 00:08:35,951
What happened next?

33
00:08:36,031 --> 00:08:36,871
Was anyone there?

34
00:08:36,951 --> 00:08:37,781
Was it warm?

35
00:08:37,871 --> 00:08:39,871
Was it bigger than Iceland?

36
00:08:39,951 --> 00:08:45,831
Yeah. It was much bigger than this place.
There was grass growing everywhere.

37
00:08:50,461 --> 00:08:52,511
We saw some Natives too.

38
00:08:54,011 --> 00:08:57,511
We couldn't understand each other,
but we became friends.

39
00:08:58,681 --> 00:09:02,641
This feather headdress and pipe are gifts from them.

40
00:09:03,311 --> 00:09:05,061
Listen. See this thing here?

41
00:09:05,851 --> 00:09:09,941
Only true warriors are allowed to wear it.

42
00:09:11,071 --> 00:09:14,111
Well, you know...

43
00:09:16,861 --> 00:09:18,071
That's amazing.

44
00:09:19,121 --> 00:09:21,291
What? The kids are still awake?

45
00:09:21,371 --> 00:09:22,291
Can I touch it?

46
00:09:22,371 --> 00:09:26,711
Sorry. I wanted them to hear Leif's stories.

47
00:09:27,081 --> 00:09:30,711
A true warrior? You really are amazing.

48
00:09:30,791 --> 00:09:36,801
Right? You're a good boy, Thorfinn.
Why don't you come sail with me when you grow up?

49
00:09:36,881 --> 00:09:38,591
What? I want to sail with you now.

50
00:09:38,931 --> 00:09:40,221
Now?

51
00:09:40,301 --> 00:09:42,101
Something's not right, Leif.

52
00:09:42,851 --> 00:09:44,601
My grandfather told me

53
00:09:45,021 --> 00:09:49,771
that Jormungand lives in the west,
and eats any ship that dares approach.

54
00:09:49,861 --> 00:09:52,231
And beyond that, there's nothing.

55
00:09:52,771 --> 00:09:57,491
That's exactly why you should realize
how amazing I am.

56
00:09:57,571 --> 00:10:00,911
See? This is the proof. I'm a true warrior.

57
00:10:00,991 --> 00:10:03,621
Leif, you're not a warrior.

58
00:10:03,701 --> 00:10:05,871
What? You don't know anything.

59
00:10:05,951 --> 00:10:08,581
The battlefield isn't the only place
where warriors are found.

60
00:10:09,081 --> 00:10:12,711
Sailors fight against the sea. They're amazing.

61
00:10:13,921 --> 00:10:15,091
I knew it.

62
00:10:15,171 --> 00:10:16,011
Yeah.

63
00:10:16,091 --> 00:10:17,131
What?

64
00:10:17,801 --> 00:10:21,351
Everyone says you're just like a kid with a beard.

65
00:10:21,431 --> 00:10:24,101
You're always going on adventures,
and you're useless in battle.

66
00:10:25,221 --> 00:10:29,441
You just can't think for yourself.
Thorfinn, you believe me, right?

67
00:10:32,151 --> 00:10:36,821
Do you mean I'm just a wandering old man
who can't fight?

68
00:10:36,901 --> 00:10:38,741
You don't believe me?

69
00:10:38,821 --> 00:10:44,491
Well, it's true that my father is a warrior...

70
00:10:49,251 --> 00:10:54,341
Thors. These kids think I'm a fool.
Beat some sense into them.

71
00:10:54,421 --> 00:10:56,551
All right. That's enough for today.

72
00:10:57,841 --> 00:11:00,341
Ari, send them home.

73
00:11:00,431 --> 00:11:01,891
Yes, Thors.

74
00:11:02,641 --> 00:11:03,721
See you later.

75
00:11:03,801 --> 00:11:04,891
Bye.

76
00:11:22,321 --> 00:11:25,951
I see. Several people froze to death in Greenland.

77
00:11:26,281 --> 00:11:30,461
Yes. The cold wave will be severe this year.
A lot of livestock will die.

78
00:11:31,121 --> 00:11:36,041
The problem is that it gets harder
to endure the cold as you get older.

79
00:11:38,591 --> 00:11:41,591
It's so cold.

80
00:11:42,131 --> 00:11:43,261
Thanks.

81
00:11:44,051 --> 00:11:46,601
These two are the only ones getting weak.

82
00:11:47,061 --> 00:11:51,271
You must be cold, Ylva. I'll warm you up.

83
00:11:51,351 --> 00:11:52,481
You pervert.

84
00:11:53,061 --> 00:11:55,861
Mother, go to sleep. You'll catch a cold again.

85
00:11:55,941 --> 00:11:59,611
-I'm fine. Can you help me tie up the sheep?
-Okay.

86
00:12:03,571 --> 00:12:05,781
Looks like life is tough here too.

87
00:12:06,451 --> 00:12:07,371
Yeah.

88
00:12:08,241 --> 00:12:11,621
Someday we might get a winter so cold
that no one survives.

89
00:12:12,041 --> 00:12:16,501
Back in my country,
my brother is making plans to emigrate right now.

90
00:12:17,711 --> 00:12:18,841
It's far, right?

91
00:12:20,261 --> 00:12:21,171
What?

92
00:12:22,301 --> 00:12:24,511
I made the round trip.

93
00:12:28,971 --> 00:12:32,561
Go. Vinland is near.

94
00:12:32,891 --> 00:12:34,981
-Captain Thorfinn.
-What?

95
00:12:35,731 --> 00:12:37,981
It's Jormungand.

96
00:12:38,061 --> 00:12:40,941
Bring it on, you monster.

97
00:12:46,781 --> 00:12:48,031
That hurts.

98
00:12:52,501 --> 00:12:55,921
Sister. Sleep in your own bed.
There's not enough room.

99
00:12:56,001 --> 00:12:58,711
What? I don't want to.

100
00:12:59,131 --> 00:13:02,341
That's not fair. I'm the one who warmed up this bed.

101
00:13:04,761 --> 00:13:07,761
They're still talking.

102
00:13:09,971 --> 00:13:14,101
Don't pay attention to them. Worrying is for adults.

103
00:13:14,981 --> 00:13:18,981
Come over here. I'll give you a hug.

104
00:13:26,701 --> 00:13:32,581
Hey, Father. Why can't we get a slave?

105
00:13:33,201 --> 00:13:36,921
Mother's health is poor.
And now I have to help with the chores.

106
00:13:37,001 --> 00:13:40,041
That's not what we do.
Stop asking the same thing every year.

107
00:13:42,711 --> 00:13:44,761
You're so weird.

108
00:13:51,141 --> 00:13:52,141
Ylva.

109
00:13:53,181 --> 00:13:56,771
I thought I was going to die.

110
00:13:57,021 --> 00:13:58,021
Are you hurt?

111
00:13:58,101 --> 00:13:59,151
I'm fine.

112
00:14:00,361 --> 00:14:01,401
What's wrong?

113
00:14:01,901 --> 00:14:03,941
There's something under here.

114
00:14:06,781 --> 00:14:07,651
Ylva?

115
00:14:34,721 --> 00:14:38,351
Hey, old man.
How much of your story yesterday is true?

116
00:14:38,851 --> 00:14:41,351
What are you talking about? It's all true.

117
00:14:42,111 --> 00:14:46,861
Then take me on this ship.
I'll believe it if I see it with my own eyes.

118
00:14:46,941 --> 00:14:50,861
Idiot. That's not what it means to believe.

119
00:14:50,951 --> 00:14:54,621
If you want to ride a boat, then go ask your father.

120
00:14:54,951 --> 00:14:58,961
Our warship is sitting over there.
Our village has been using it for many generations.

121
00:14:59,041 --> 00:15:01,791
I'm sure your father would be happy to lend it to you.

122
00:15:03,211 --> 00:15:06,841
My father's so stingy. He always says no,
no matter how many times I ask.

123
00:15:07,461 --> 00:15:09,301
He's not being stingy.

124
00:15:10,631 --> 00:15:14,551
The ocean here is scary, Thorfinn.

125
00:15:16,011 --> 00:15:19,561
I got trapped in an ice floe once, when I was young.

126
00:15:20,231 --> 00:15:24,771
I had just moved to Greenland. I was careless.

127
00:15:25,321 --> 00:15:30,401
We ran out of food in no time.
We ate ice to make us forget our hunger.

128
00:15:31,451 --> 00:15:34,071
Our boat was finally crushed by the ice.

129
00:15:34,741 --> 00:15:38,081
We were left with no choice but to walk on the sea.

130
00:15:41,501 --> 00:15:44,671
And then one day, I noticed I was the only one left.

131
00:15:46,541 --> 00:15:49,841
My six companions were all dead.

132
00:15:54,141 --> 00:15:55,221
Why?

133
00:15:56,551 --> 00:16:01,311
Why do we live so far north,
if we have to go through all this?

134
00:16:05,271 --> 00:16:08,781
Helga. Boil some water. Ylva. Bring the fur blanket.

135
00:16:08,861 --> 00:16:09,821
Okay.

136
00:16:11,111 --> 00:16:13,151
Oh, no. What happened?

137
00:16:13,241 --> 00:16:14,781
He was buried in the snow.

138
00:16:23,581 --> 00:16:26,671
That looks like it hurts. What happened to him?

139
00:16:27,341 --> 00:16:28,551
Add more wood to the fire.

140
00:16:28,631 --> 00:16:29,751
Okay.

141
00:16:30,921 --> 00:16:36,341
Hey, Father. Isn't he already dead?
His hands and feet have already rotted.

142
00:16:36,721 --> 00:16:40,471
That guy's dangerous.

143
00:16:41,431 --> 00:16:47,061
He's not from our village. Poor thing.
Why was he outside in the blizzard?

144
00:16:47,861 --> 00:16:49,611
Why?

145
00:16:50,821 --> 00:16:55,451
He's a runaway slave.
You'll get in trouble if you hide him.

146
00:17:00,791 --> 00:17:02,251
We ran away.

147
00:17:02,751 --> 00:17:06,621
My ancestors used to live much further
to the east, a long time ago.

148
00:17:07,421 --> 00:17:09,631
In a land called Norway.

149
00:17:10,551 --> 00:17:13,471
It's warmer than here. The sea doesn't freeze.

150
00:17:14,131 --> 00:17:18,051
It's a good land, protected by many coves.

151
00:17:19,051 --> 00:17:23,851
But one day, a powerful man named Harald appeared.

152
00:17:24,891 --> 00:17:28,981
Harald's forces conquered our villages
like a raging fire.

153
00:17:29,611 --> 00:17:34,151
He proclaimed himself to be the first king of Norway
and became its ruler.

154
00:17:34,861 --> 00:17:36,651
He forced the people to choose

155
00:17:37,491 --> 00:17:42,831
whether they would obey the king, or leave Norway.

156
00:17:43,871 --> 00:17:46,921
Many people hated Harald's tyranny.

157
00:17:47,421 --> 00:17:49,791
They moved to a faraway land,
away from Harald's reach.

158
00:17:51,001 --> 00:17:54,171
They came here, to Iceland.

159
00:17:56,471 --> 00:17:57,551
You're lying.

160
00:18:00,091 --> 00:18:01,891
You're a liar.

161
00:18:02,351 --> 00:18:05,271
My father and my ancestors would never run away.

162
00:18:05,681 --> 00:18:08,771
Stupid old man. You wandering old coot.

163
00:18:19,111 --> 00:18:20,491
Father.

164
00:18:22,031 --> 00:18:25,751
Father. Listen to me. Leif said...

165
00:18:35,131 --> 00:18:36,591
Welcome back, Thorfinn.

166
00:18:37,631 --> 00:18:39,261
Who is he?

167
00:18:39,801 --> 00:18:42,261
He's someone else's slave. He ran away.

168
00:18:45,851 --> 00:18:47,311
He came back to life.

169
00:18:47,981 --> 00:18:50,441
Hey, you. Can you say your name?

170
00:18:53,361 --> 00:18:54,401
I'm so cold.

171
00:18:54,981 --> 00:18:57,361
The fire. Ylva, get the fire going.

172
00:18:57,441 --> 00:18:58,781
Oh, okay.

173
00:18:59,451 --> 00:19:01,531
Where did you come from?

174
00:19:05,451 --> 00:19:07,251
Are you going to make me go back...

175
00:19:08,291 --> 00:19:10,541
to Halfdan's household?

176
00:19:11,041 --> 00:19:14,211
Halfdan? You came from the other side
of the mountains?

177
00:19:16,301 --> 00:19:17,841
I hate it there.

178
00:19:18,881 --> 00:19:19,801
I hate it.

179
00:19:22,051 --> 00:19:22,891
But...

180
00:19:24,601 --> 00:19:25,761
I don't...

181
00:19:26,931 --> 00:19:28,891
have anywhere else to go.

182
00:19:34,361 --> 00:19:37,821
Thors. Come. Halfdan is down at the harbor.

183
00:19:38,191 --> 00:19:42,821
Those guys came to pick a fight again.
You're the only one who can handle him.

184
00:19:43,491 --> 00:19:46,871
They said something about a slave.
They forced their way onto shore.

185
00:19:47,291 --> 00:19:52,001
Leif is trying to intervene,
but I'm afraid he'll start a fight.

186
00:19:52,581 --> 00:19:53,881
Just come.

187
00:20:00,261 --> 00:20:04,181
Chains are the only type of necklace
that looks good on a human.

188
00:20:04,761 --> 00:20:06,261
Not gold. Not silver.

189
00:20:07,141 --> 00:20:08,721
An iron chain.

190
00:20:10,351 --> 00:20:15,441
A chain around a man's neck
is the only way to make him half decent.

191
00:20:18,981 --> 00:20:20,531
Don't you agree?

192
00:20:22,991 --> 00:20:27,281
Last night, one of my slaves cut his chain
and ran away.

193
00:20:27,701 --> 00:20:29,161
Do you know anything about this?

194
00:20:29,991 --> 00:20:31,871
Stop trying to pick a fight with us.

195
00:20:32,831 --> 00:20:35,631
You're just trying to drag up old fights again.

196
00:20:36,081 --> 00:20:38,841
-We've had problems with him before.
-What a bunch of sissies.

197
00:20:38,921 --> 00:20:42,011
-We had a mediation the other day.
-You think your numbers scare us?

198
00:20:42,421 --> 00:20:45,471
You brat. I remember you.

199
00:20:46,141 --> 00:20:48,891
You cut off my left arm during that one fight.

200
00:20:49,221 --> 00:20:53,271
How dare you cut off my brother's leg?

201
00:20:53,351 --> 00:20:55,651
Stop, Ari.

202
00:20:55,731 --> 00:21:00,271
My brother will never be able to walk again.
I'm going to chop off your leg.

203
00:21:00,361 --> 00:21:04,201
Bring it on. Who cares about the law?
Who cares about the town assembly?

204
00:21:09,451 --> 00:21:10,831
Halfdan...

205
00:21:11,201 --> 00:21:13,911
I'm going to tie you up, you bastard.

206
00:21:24,511 --> 00:21:27,091
I won't let anyone get away with breaking the law.

207
00:21:27,761 --> 00:21:29,681
The law is a chain.

208
00:21:29,761 --> 00:21:34,101
The only way to make a heretic a human is
to bind him with the law.

209
00:21:35,351 --> 00:21:36,771
Don't forget.

210
00:21:41,321 --> 00:21:44,531
The runaway slave is a young Slav.

211
00:21:45,191 --> 00:21:47,361
Have you seen him?

212
00:21:53,581 --> 00:21:56,161
Where am I?

213
00:22:01,671 --> 00:22:05,841
I've never felt so peaceful before.

214
00:22:07,051 --> 00:22:09,301
Hang in there. Hey, you.

215
00:22:09,721 --> 00:22:11,431
Stay awake. Or you'll die.

216
00:22:12,351 --> 00:22:13,511
Thors.

217
00:22:16,311 --> 00:22:17,441
Dad.

218
00:22:19,311 --> 00:22:22,691
Mom. Sister.

219
00:22:23,361 --> 00:22:26,241
You were all here.

220
00:22:26,861 --> 00:22:28,911
I was worried about you.

221
00:22:29,411 --> 00:22:31,531
We all got sold to different places.

222
00:22:32,581 --> 00:22:35,081
Where am I?

223
00:22:36,121 --> 00:22:38,331
Why am I...?

224
00:22:43,801 --> 00:22:45,381
Far, far to the west,

225
00:22:46,051 --> 00:22:50,681
across the sea, there's a land called Vinland.

226
00:22:52,221 --> 00:22:53,721
It's warm

227
00:22:55,011 --> 00:22:55,931
and fertile.

228
00:22:56,681 --> 00:23:01,401
It's a faraway land,
without slave traders and without war.

229
00:23:02,861 --> 00:23:03,941
What do you think?

230
00:23:05,861 --> 00:23:10,241
Come live there with us someday.

231
00:23:12,411 --> 00:23:13,701
Vinland...

232
00:23:17,451 --> 00:23:18,581
I see...

233
00:23:21,791 --> 00:23:26,001
This land is called Vinland.

234
00:23:37,971 --> 00:23:41,691
I found him. Pardon my bad manners, Thors.

235
00:23:42,351 --> 00:23:45,571
I'm the rightful owner of that slave.

236
00:23:45,651 --> 00:23:47,151
Give him back to me.

237
00:23:48,651 --> 00:23:50,151
Father...

238
00:23:50,241 --> 00:23:51,571
Come, Thorfinn.

239
00:23:52,611 --> 00:23:53,781
Halfdan.

240
00:23:55,241 --> 00:23:56,411
You know...

241
00:23:56,831 --> 00:23:58,701
We weren't trying to hide him.

242
00:23:59,201 --> 00:24:02,711
We saved your slave when he was about to die.

243
00:24:02,791 --> 00:24:06,001
Halfdan. How much did you pay for this man?

244
00:24:09,211 --> 00:24:13,051
Father, it's rude to ask that.

245
00:24:13,131 --> 00:24:15,181
Two sheep. Both young ewes.

246
00:24:15,851 --> 00:24:17,931
I'll give you three sheep. I'll buy him.

247
00:24:20,061 --> 00:24:21,681
What do you think, Halfdan?

248
00:24:23,141 --> 00:24:25,441
I'm bringing him back.

249
00:24:25,521 --> 00:24:29,231
I have to torture him in front of the other slaves
to make sure they learn their lesson.

250
00:24:33,911 --> 00:24:35,701
Then I'll give you four sheep.

251
00:24:36,951 --> 00:24:39,951
You bastard. Didn't you hear what Halfdan said?

252
00:24:40,041 --> 00:24:41,041
Shut up.

253
00:24:43,211 --> 00:24:45,121
What if I said I want twice that many?

254
00:24:46,461 --> 00:24:50,051
I'll pay. I'll give you all ewes. Under 3 years old.

255
00:24:56,391 --> 00:24:59,431
Eight sheep for a man who's good as dead?

256
00:25:03,181 --> 00:25:04,981
That was a good deal.

257
00:25:06,981 --> 00:25:10,321
No way. The deal's closed?

258
00:25:13,861 --> 00:25:18,321
I bet you think you freed him from his chains.

259
00:25:19,331 --> 00:25:21,871
Where are you going to take him now?

260
00:25:26,371 --> 00:25:29,421
I'll be the one to choose the sheep.

261
00:25:44,481 --> 00:25:46,851
Sister. Are you sad?

262
00:25:46,941 --> 00:25:52,021
Of course I'm sad. He cost eight sheep.
But he died right afterwards.

263
00:25:53,151 --> 00:25:57,781
Father, you idiot.

264
00:25:57,861 --> 00:25:58,861
Father.

265
00:26:00,531 --> 00:26:02,991
Did we run away too?

266
00:26:03,581 --> 00:26:04,911
Leif told me...

267
00:26:05,791 --> 00:26:08,961
that our ancestors fled here from the east.

268
00:26:10,211 --> 00:26:12,711
He said they ran away and came here.

269
00:26:14,051 --> 00:26:16,971
Yes. That's what they say.

270
00:26:21,891 --> 00:26:22,971
Then...

271
00:26:29,561 --> 00:26:33,401
Then where does everyone
who wants to run away from here...

272
00:26:35,611 --> 00:26:36,901
go?
